===== STATUS: success =====
Question: Can large language models solve university mathematics and computer science course questions?

Large language models demonstrate substantial capability in solving university-level mathematics and computer science course questions. When evaluated on MIT's comprehensive curriculum, GPT-3.5 successfully solves approximately one-third of the entire dataset comprising 4,550 questions from Mathematics and EECS courses (zhang2023exploringthemit pages 1-3). GPT-4 achieves notably superior performance, reaching perfect accuracy on test sets that exclude image-based questions when combined with prompt engineering techniques (zhang2023exploringthemit pages 1-3).

The effectiveness of LLMs on such tasks is substantially enhanced through strategic prompting methodologies. Few-shot learning, chain-of-thought reasoning, and novel "expert prompting" approaches all improve model performance (zhang2023exploringthemit pages 1-3). Additionally, fine-tuned open-source models demonstrate improved performance on reasoning-intensive tasks (zhang2023exploringthemit pages 1-3).

Beyond course-specific evaluation, LLMs exhibit strong performance on specialized academic tasks. The Reflexion framework demonstrates that language agents can solve programming problems at 91% pass@1 on the HumanEval benchmark and achieve 20% improvements on reasoning tasks like HotPotQA through self-reflection mechanisms (shinn2023reflexionlanguageagents pages 1-2).

In summary, contemporary LLMs—particularly GPT-4—can effectively solve university-level mathematics and computer science questions, with performance varying based on model architecture, prompting strategy, and question modality.

